Import the face detection cascade
To import the required cascade, we use the cv2.CascadeClassifier() function of the OpenCV library. This function requires the location of the cascade file as its parameter:

faceCascade = cv2.CascadeClassifier("/usercode/haarcascades/haarcascade_frontalface_alt2.xml")
We need the haarcascade_frontalface_alt2.xml file to detect the faces.

Detect faces
To detect faces, we use the faceCascade.detectMultiScale()` function of the OpenCV library. We can access this function from the cascade that we imported. This function requires three parameters, which are listed below:

faces = faceCascade.detectMultiScale(imgGray, scaleFactor, minNeighbour)
imgGray is the grayscale image to detect faces.

scaleFactor is the parameter to specify how much the image is reduced at each image scale. For our image, 1.1 is the ideal value.

minNeighbour is the parameter to specify how many neighbors each candidate rectangle should have to retain. Its ideal value is between 3 and 6.

Draw contours
After detecting the faces, the faces variable will store the x-coordinates, y-coordinates, width, and height of the image. We use the for loop for all the faces detected, get their respective parameters, and use the cv2.rectangle() function to draw rectangular contours around the faces detected. This function requires five parameters:

for (x, y, w, h) in faces:
    cv2.rectangle(resizedImage, (x, y), (x + w, y + h), (0, 0, 255), 2)

